# Production Admin Dashboard Environment Variables
#
# This file documents all environment variables required for production deployment
# of the admin dashboard. DO NOT commit secrets to the repository.
#
# Copy this file and populate with actual values in the Railway production environment.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN API AUTHENTICATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Required. The shared secret used to authenticate requests to admin endpoints.
# Must be a strong random value (64+ alphanumeric characters).
#
# Generation:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   # or
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
#
# All requests to /api/admin/* must include this key in the x-admin-api-key header:
#   curl -H "x-admin-api-key: $ADMIN_API_KEY" https://memwal.ai/api/admin/wallets
#
ADMIN_API_KEY=<generate-strong-random-64-char-secret>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BALANCE MONITORING & ALERTING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Interval (in seconds) for the background balance monitor job to execute.
# Recommended: 900 (15 minutes)
# Minimum: 60 (1 minute, not recommended for production)
# Maximum: 86400 (24 hours)
#
# The job:
#   1. Fetches wallet balances from sidecar every BALANCE_MONITOR_INTERVAL_SECS
#   2. Compares against configured thresholds
#   3. Sends Slack alert if balance falls below threshold
#   4. Deduplicates alerts (max 1 per wallet per hour)
BALANCE_MONITOR_INTERVAL_SECS=900

# Alert threshold for uploader pool WAL balance (in frost, 1 WAL = 10^9 frost).
# Alert fires when uploader pool WAL balance falls BELOW this value.
#
# Recommended production values:
#   - Conservative: 500000 (0.5 WAL - minimal operations)
#   - Moderate: 1000000 (1 WAL - standard production)
#   - Lenient: 5000000 (5 WAL - high capacity)
#
# Set based on:
#   - Daily WAL burn rate for uploads
#   - Risk tolerance for service degradation
#   - Frequency of manual replenishment
#
# Example: If daily burn is ~10 WAL, set threshold to at least 200 WAL (20 days buffer)
WALLET_BALANCE_LOW_THRESHOLD_WAL=500000

# Alert threshold for sponsor wallet SUI balance (in mist, 1 SUI = 10^9 mist).
# Alert fires when sponsor wallet SUI balance falls BELOW this value.
#
# Recommended production values:
#   - Conservative: 10000000 (0.01 SUI - minimal gas only)
#   - Moderate: 50000000 (0.05 SUI - standard production)
#   - Lenient: 100000000 (0.1 SUI - high capacity)
#
# Set based on:
#   - Daily SUI gas consumption
#   - Cost of emergency refills
#   - Risk tolerance for execution failures
#
# Note: Sponsor wallet is used for gas fees on Sui blockchain transactions.
# Monitor closely as gas prices can spike unexpectedly.
SPONSOR_BALANCE_LOW_THRESHOLD_SUI=50000000

# Slack Webhook URL for balance alerts.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Go to Slack workspace → Create an Incoming Webhook
#   2. Point webhook to #alerts or dedicated admin channel
#   3. Copy the full HTTPS URL
#   4. Paste here
#
# Format: https://hooks.slack.com/services/<TEAM_ID>/<WEBHOOK_ID>/<TOKEN>
#
# When a balance falls below threshold, a formatted alert appears in Slack with:
#   - Wallet address
#   - Current balance
#   - Alert threshold
#   - Timestamp
#   - Recommended actions
#
# Leave unset to disable Slack alerting (alerts still log to application logs).
ALERT_TO_SLACK=https://hooks.slack.com/services/prod-channel-webhook-url

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUI NETWORK CONFIGURATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Must match the Sui network where your wallets are funded.
# For production MemWal, this should always be "mainnet".
#
# Allowed values: testnet, mainnet, devnet, localnet
SUI_NETWORK=mainnet

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WALRUS STORAGE CONFIGURATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Publisher endpoint used for new memory uploads.
# Must correspond to SUI_NETWORK setting.
WALRUS_PUBLISHER_URL=https://publisher.walrus-mainnet.walrus.space

# Aggregator endpoint used for retrieving stored memories.
# Must correspond to SUI_NETWORK setting.
WALRUS_AGGREGATOR_URL=https://aggregator.walrus-mainnet.walrus.space

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOGGING & OBSERVABILITY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Rust logging filter for application logs.
# Use structured JSON logs in production for machine-readable output.
#
# Format:
#   RUST_LOG=<module>=<level>[,<module>=<level>...]
#   Levels: trace, debug, info, warn, error
#
# Production recommended:
#   memwal_server=info,tower_http=info
#   This logs all info+ messages from the server and HTTP middleware
RUST_LOG=memwal_server=info,tower_http=info

# Log output format.
# Options:
#   - "pretty": Human-readable (for development, harder to parse)
#   - "json": Machine-readable structured logs (recommended for production)
#
# JSON logs include: timestamp, level, module, message, request_id, latency, status_code
LOG_FORMAT=json

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CORS & SECURITY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to make cross-origin requests.
# Admin API is typically only called by backend dashboards/scripts, not browsers.
# If you're using the browser admin dashboard, include its origin here.
#
# Production examples:
#   - ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://memwal.ai,https://admin.memwal.ai
#   - ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://memwal.ai
#   - Leave unset for deny-all (safest for API-only deployment)
#
# If unset (default), CORS is deny-all (browsers will see CORS errors).
# This is the safe default for a backend-only service.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://memwal.ai

# CORS is configured per route. Admin routes typically don't need CORS
# if called exclusively from server-to-server (not browser).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE CONFIGURATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# PostgreSQL connection URL for the production database.
#
# Format:
#   postgresql://username:password@host:port/database
#
# Required fields:
#   - username: Database user (usually "memwal")
#   - password: Database password
#   - host: PostgreSQL server hostname or IP
#   - port: PostgreSQL port (usually 5432)
#   - database: Database name (usually "memwal")
#
# For Railway-managed PostgreSQL:
#   railway variable get DATABASE_URL  # Retrieve the auto-generated URL
#
# Example:
#   postgresql://memwal:secret@db.railway.internal:5432/memwal
#
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://memwal:password@db.railway.internal:5432/memwal

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL MONITORING & DEBUGGING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Enable query logging for debugging slow admin queries (optional).
# Set to "true" to log all SQL queries and execution times.
# ENABLE_QUERY_LOG=false

# Maximum number of database connections in the pool (optional).
# Increase if you get "unable to acquire connection" errors.
# Default is auto-configured based on available resources.
# SQLX_MAX_CONNECTIONS=20

# OpenTelemetry export endpoint for distributed tracing (optional).
# If set, traces are exported to this endpoint for visualization in Jaeger/Datadog.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEPLOYMENT TAGS (RECOMMENDED BUT OPTIONAL)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These tags help with debugging and monitoring in production.
# Include them in your deployment for better observability.

# Environment identifier
DEPLOYMENT_ENVIRONMENT=production

# AWS region or deployment region
DEPLOYMENT_REGION=us-east-1

# Current git commit SHA (set at deployment time)
DEPLOYMENT_VERSION=<git-commit-sha>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECURITY CHECKLIST
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Before deploying with this config, verify:
#
# [ ] ADMIN_API_KEY is 64+ random characters (alphanumeric, no special chars)
#     Use: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# [ ] ADMIN_API_KEY is NOT committed to git
#     Verify: git log -p -- docs/admin-dashboard/.env | grep ADMIN_API_KEY
#
# [ ] SUI_NETWORK is set to "mainnet" (not testnet or localnet)
#     Verify: echo $SUI_NETWORK
#
# [ ] WALRUS_* URLs point to mainnet endpoints (not testnet)
#     Verify: grep -i "walrus-mainnet" docs/admin-dashboard/.env.production.example
#
# [ ] ALERT_TO_SLACK webhook is valid (test with curl)
#     Test: curl -X POST -d '{"text":"Test"}' $ALERT_TO_SLACK
#
# [ ] DATABASE_URL points to production database (not staging/local)
#     Verify: echo $DATABASE_URL | grep -i production
#
# [ ] LOG_FORMAT is set to "json" (not "pretty")
#     Verify: echo $LOG_FORMAT
#
# [ ] Slack alerts channel exists and webhook has permission
#     Check: Go to Slack channel → Integrations → Incoming Webhooks
#
# [ ] Wallet balance thresholds are appropriate for production capacity
#     Review: Calculate daily burn rate, set threshold to 2-4 weeks of burn
#
# [ ] All required environment variables are set (none are commented out)
#     Verify: railway variable list | wc -l
#
# [ ] Staging has been tested and verified working before production deploy
#     Verify: Check recent staging deployment logs
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEPLOYMENT PROCEDURE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Set all variables in Railway production environment:
#    railway variable set ADMIN_API_KEY=<secret>
#    railway variable set BALANCE_MONITOR_INTERVAL_SECS=900
#    ... (set all others)
#
# 2. Verify all variables are set:
#    railway variable list
#
# 3. Deploy relayer service:
#    cd services/server
#    railway up --service relayer
#
# 4. Watch deployment logs:
#    railway logs --service relayer -f
#
# 5. Verify deployment succeeded:
#    curl -H "x-admin-api-key: $(railway variable get ADMIN_API_KEY)" \
#      https://memwal.ai/api/admin/config
#
# 6. Monitor for 10 minutes:
#    railway logs --service relayer -f | grep -i "error\|failed"
#
# See DEPLOYMENT.md for detailed step-by-step procedure.
